  gen_random_uuid(),
  'Wall Pilates Class For Beginners',
  '<h2>Welcome to Wall Pilates!</h2>
<p>I specialize in guiding you towards achieving optimal core strength and balance. Pilates is a well-known fitness regimen aimed at enhancing flexibility, core strength and body awareness. We''ve curated a selection of Wall Pilates exercises designed to intensify your workout while making it fun and innovative. Let''s jump right in!</p>

<h2>Wall Side Knee Bends</h2>
<p>Lay on your back with your glutes next to a wall. The soles of your feet on the wall and your knees parallel to the floor. Your arms are by your side. Keep the knees together and feet on the wall as you rotate both knees to your left, aiming to touch the left thigh on the wall. Keep the knees in line with your hips. Repeat this movement rotating the knees to the right, aiming to touch the right thigh on the wall. Inhale when you rotate to the left. Exhale when you rotate to the right. This movement mobilizes the hip joint, as well as the lower back so you must perform this action in a slow and controlled manner.</p>

<h2>Wall Butterfly Pose</h2>
<p>Lay on the floor. Your glutes are pushed against a wall with the soles of your feet together on a wall and your knees forming a V shape. Hold this position, then slowly bring the feet together so that the soles of the feet are on the wall, and that arms are together in front of your chest. Repeat this movement. Aiming to get the knees further to the wall each time with each pulse. Inhale when the knees are apart. Exhale when the knees are together. This is a stretch for the groin so this movement needs to be performed under control.</p>

<h2>Wall Open Armed Bended Knee</h2>
<p>Lay on your back with your glutes next to a wall. Straighten your right leg so that it is pointing up to the ceiling. Lower the right leg outwards, aiming to touch the right foot onto the floor to the right of you. When you feel tension in the right adductor, return to the starting position. Repeat for the required repetitions. Inhale when you lower the leg. Exhale when you raise the leg. This movement mobilizes the hip joint, as well as stretching the lower back.</p>

<h2>Wall Straddle Stretch</h2>
<p>Lay on the floor. Your glutes are pushed against a wall, with your legs 90 degrees to your body resting against the wall. Brace your abdominals, keep the legs against the wall as you separate the legs until you feel a stretch in the inner thigh. Hold for a moment, then return to the starting position. Inhale when the legs are apart. Exhale as you bring the legs together. Try to keep your back as straight as possible and lower back on the floor throughout.</p>

<h2>Reach Through Series</h2>
<p>Lay on your back with your glutes next to a wall. The soles of your feet on the wall and your knees parallel to the floor. Your arms are extended overhead. Brace the abdominals as you lift your arms and torso off the floor, squeezing your abdominals at the top, reaching your hands in between your legs. Return to the starting position. Inhale when you are in the starting position. Exhale when you perform the stomach crunch. You should feel tension in the abdominals overall.</p>

<h2>Butterfly Openers</h2>
<p>Lay on the floor. Your glutes are pushed against a wall with the soles of your feet together on a wall and your knees forming a V shape. Your arms are out to the sides, level with your shoulders and palms pointing up to the ceiling. Slowly bring the feet and arms together so that the soles of the feet are on the wall and arms are together in front of your chest. Inhale when the arms and knees are apart. Exhale when the arms and knees are together.</p>

<h2>Alternating Side Hip Slides</h2>
<p>Lay on your back with your glutes next to a wall. Your legs are resting against the wall, pointing up to the ceiling. Lower the right leg outwards while lowering your right arm, aiming to touch the floor. When you feel tension in the right adductor, return both arms and legs to the starting position. Repeat with the left arm and left leg. Inhale when you lower. Exhale when you raise. This movement mobilizes the hip joint, opens the chest, and stretches the lower back.</p>

<h2>Alternating Leg Abduction</h2>
<p>Lay on your back with your glutes next to a wall. The soles of your feet are on the wall and your knees parallel to the floor. Your arms are on the floor level with your shoulders. Brace the abdominals as you squeeze your right glute to lower your right leg towards the floor, keeping the angle in your right leg at all times. Return to the starting position. Repeat with the left leg. Inhale at the starting position. Exhale when you perform the leg abduction. You should feel tension in the glutes overall.</p>

<h2>Opposite Toe Reach</h2>
<p>Sit on the floor with your legs straight out in front of you, your back against a wall and your hands by your ears. As you breathe out, lean forward from your hips, keeping your back as straight as possible, aiming to touch your left toes with your right hand. Return to the starting position. Repeat leaning forward to touch your right toes with your left hand. Inhale at the start. Exhale as you lean forward. You should feel this in your lower back and hamstrings.</p>

<h2>Seated Active Forward Fold</h2>
<p>Sit on the floor with your legs straight out in front of you, and your back to the wall. As you breathe out, lean forward from your hips, keeping your back as straight as possible, aiming to touch your toes with your hands. Hold for a second, then return to the starting position. Inhale at the start. Exhale as you lean forward. Keep your back as straight as possible throughout the movement.</p>',
